WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.000
Pirates.of.the.Caribbean-
[2006]DvDrip[Eng]-aXXo-FixMan sync+


00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.200
Will!


00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.600
- Why is this happening?
- I don't know.


00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.900
You look beautiful.


00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.500
It's bad luck for the groom
to see the bride before the wedding.


00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.800
Make way! Let me through!
How dare you!


00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.200
Stand your men down at once.
Do you hear me?


00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.300
Governor Weatherby Swann,
it's been too long.


00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.800
- Cutler Beckett?
- It's Lord now, actually.


00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.600
Lord or not, you have no reason
and no authority to arrest this man.


00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.400
In fact, I do. Mr. Mercer?


00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.200
The warrant for the arrest
of one William Turner.


00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.400
- This warrant is for Elizabeth Swann.
- Oh, is it? That's annoying. My mistake.


00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.800
- Arrest her.
- On what charges?


00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.400
Aha. Here's the one for William Turner.


00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.500
And I have another one
for a Mr. James Norrington.


00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.500
- Is he present?
- What are the charges?


00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.200
Commodore Norrington resigned
his commission some months ago.


00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.600
That wasn't the answer
to the question I asked.


00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.200
Lord Beckett. In the category
of questions not answered...


00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.600
We are under the jurisdiction
of the king's governor of Port Royal


00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:43.600
and you will tell us
what we are charged with.
The charge is "conspiring to set free


00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.300
"a man convicted of crimes
against the Crown and Empire


00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.000
"and condemned to death,
for which the..."


00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.600
For which the punishment,
regrettably, is also death.


00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.900
Perhaps you remember a certain pirate
named Jack Sparrow.
Captain.


00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.600
Captain Jack Sparrow.


00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.400
Captain Jack Sparrow.


00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.600
Yes, I thought you might.


00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.500
# Fifteen men
on a dead man's chest


00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:22.200
# Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum


00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.400
# Drink and the devil
had done for the rest


00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.900
# Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum


00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.600
Prisoners, come on!


00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.600
Sorry, mate.


00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:36.700
Mind if we make a little side trip?


00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.800
I didn't think so.


00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.500
- Not quite according to plan.
- Complications arose, ensued,


00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.500
were overcome.


00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:56.200
- You got what you went in for, then?
- Mm-hm.


00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.500
Captain, I think the crew,
meaning me as well,


00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.800
were expecting something
a bit more... shiny,


00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:09.000
with the Isla de Muerta going pear-shaped,
reclaimed by the sea with the treasure.


00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.400
And the Royal Navy chasing us
around the Atlantic.


00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.100
- And the hurricane.
- Aye.


00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.800
All in all, it seems some time
since we did a speck of honest pirating.


00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.600
- Shiny.
- Aye, shiny.


00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.700
Is that how you're all feeling?


00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:32.000
That perhaps dear old Jack is not serving
your best interests as captain?


00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.100
Walk the plank.


00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.100
- What did the bird say?
- Do not blame the bird.


00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.400
Show us what is on
that piece of cloth there.


00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.700
- You know that don't do no good.
- It does me.


00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.800
- It's a key.
- No. Much more better.


00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.600
It is a drawing of a key.


00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:08.600
Gentlemen,


00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.500
what do keys do?


00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.300
Keys...


00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.600
unlock things?


00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.400
And whatever this key unlocks,
inside there's something valuable.


00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:24.200
So we're setting out to find
whatever this key unlocks.
No.


00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:28.600
If we don't have the key, we can't open
whatever we don't have that it unlocks.


00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.300
So what purpose would be served
in finding whatever need be unlocked,


00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.800
which we don't have, without first
having found the key what unlocks it?


00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.000
So we're going after this key.


00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:42.200
You're not making any sense at all.


00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.600
Any more questions?


00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.100
So... do we have a heading?


00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.200
Ha. A heading.


00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.500
Set sail in a...


00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.300
general...


00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.700
that-way direction.


00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.700
Captain?
Come on, snap to and make sail. You
know how this works. Go on. Oi, oi, oi!


00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:14.200
Have you noticed lately


00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:18.800
the captain seems to be acting
a bit strange... er?


00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.500
Setting sail without knowing
his own heading.


00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.500
Something's got Jack vexed,
and mark my words,


00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.300
what bodes ill for Jack Sparrow
bodes ill for us all.


00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.700
Lord Beckett,
the prisoner as ordered, sir.
Those won't be necessary.


00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.900
The East India Trading Company
has need of your services.


00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.700
We wish for you to act as our agent


00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:04.800
in a business transaction
with our mutual friend Captain Sparrow.


00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:10.000
More acquaintance.
How do you know him?
We've had dealings in the past.


00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.400
And we've each
left our mark on the other.


00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.700
What mark did he leave on you?


00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.900
By your efforts,
Jack Sparrow was set free.


00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.000
I would like you to go to him


00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:29.500
and recover a certain property
in his possession.


00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.100
Recover. At the point of a sword?


00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.500
Bargain.


00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.600
Letters of marque.


00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.400
You will offer
what amounts to a full pardon.


00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.500
Jack will be free,
a privateer in the employ of England.


00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.800
Somehow I doubt Jack will consider
employment the same as being free.


00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.800
Freedom.


00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.000
Jack Sparrow is a dying breed.


00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.900
The world is shrinking,


00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:07.300
the blank edges of the map filled in.


00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:11.200
Jack must find his place
in the New World or perish.


00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.800
Not unlike you, Mr. Turner.


00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.900
You and your fiancée
face the hangman's noose.


00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:21.900
- So you get Jack and the <i>Black Pearl.</i>
- The <i>Black Pearl?</i>


00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.000
- The property you want that he possesses.
- A ship? Hardly.


00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.100
The item in question is considerably
smaller and far more valuable,


00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.500
something Sparrow keeps
on his person at all times.


00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.100
A compass?


00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.200
Ah, you know it.


00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:43.800
Bring back that compass
or there's no deal.


00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.400
Why is the rum always gone?


00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.400
Oh.


00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.000
That's why.


00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.700
As you were, gents.


00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.500
Ah.


00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.200
Time's run out, Jack.


00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.500
Bootstrap.


00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.700
Bill Turner.


00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.500
You look good, Jack.


00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.100
Is this a dream?


00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:43.500
No.


00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:47.200
I thought not.
If it were, there'd be rum.


00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:57.100
You got the <i>Pearl</i> back, I see.


00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:01.500
I had some help retrieving the <i>Pearl,</i>
by the way.


00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.900
Your son.


00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.300
William?


00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.300
He ended up a pirate after all.


00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:15.000
And to what do I owe
the pleasure of your carbuncle?


00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.100
He sent me.


00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:21.000
Davy Jones.


00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.500
Oh.


00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:25.500
So it's you, then.


00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:30.300
He shanghaied you
into service, eh?


00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.300
I chose it.


00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:37.200
I'm sorry for the part I played
in the mutiny against you, Jack.


00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.400
I stood up for you.


00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:43.300
Everything went wrong after that.


00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:49.600
They strapped me to a cannon.
I ended up on the bottom of the ocean,


00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:54.800
the weight of the water
crushing down on me.
Unable to move.


00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.000
Unable to die, Jack.


00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:03.600
And I thought that even the tiniest hope
of escaping this fate, I would take it.


00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.300
I would trade anything for it.


00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.400
It's funny what a man will do
to forestall his final judgment.


00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.900
You made a deal with him too, Jack.


00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:18.600
He raised the <i>Pearl</i>
from the depths for you.
13 years, you've been her captain.


00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:20.500
- Technically...
- Jack.


00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.900
You won't be able
to talk yourself out of this.


00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:25.600
The terms what applied to me
apply to you as well.


00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:29.900
One soul bound to crew
100 years upon his ship.


00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:34.600
The <i>Flying Dutchman</i>
already has a captain...
Then it's the locker for you!


00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.800
Jones's terrible leviathan will find you


00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:42.100
and drag the <i>Pearl</i> back to the depths
and you along with it.


00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:47.400
Any idea when Jones might release
said terrible beastie?


00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:50.200
I already told you, Jack.


00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.200
Your time is up.


00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.000
It comes now,


00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:02.300
drawn with ravenous hunger
to the man what bears the black spot.


00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.100
On deck, all hands!
Make fast the bunt gasket!


00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.400
On deck! Scurry!


00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:20.900
Scurry! I want movement!
Movement! I want movement!


00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:24.600
Lift the skin up! Keep your loof!
Haul those sheets!


00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.900
Run them! Run! Keep running! Run as if
the devil himself and itself is upon us!


00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:32.100
- Do we have a heading?
- Argh! Run! Land.


00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.800
- Which port?
- I didn't say "port," I said "land." Any land.


00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:49.900
- Jack's hat! Bring her about!
- No, no!


00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.100
Leave it!


00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:55.000
Run.


00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.900
Back to your stations,
the lot of you!


00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.600
- Jack?
- Shh.


00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:14.100
For the love of mother and child, Jack,
what's coming after us?


00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:15.700
Nothing.


00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:30.300
- Here, now, you can't be here.
- I think you'll find he can.


00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:31.700
Mr. Swann!


00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:33.800
Governor Swann, still.


00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:37.000
Do you think I wear this wig
to keep my head warm?


00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.600
Jack's compass?
What does Beckett want with that?


00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:44.600
Does it matter? I'm to find Jack
and convince him to return to Port Royal.


00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:47.100
In exchange, the charges
against us will be dropped.


00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:50.700
No. We must find our own avenue
to secure your freedom.


00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.200
Is that a lack of faith in Jack
or in me?


00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.500
That you would risk your life
to save Sparrow


00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.400
does not mean
he would do the same for anyone else.


00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.700
Now, where is that dog with the keys?


00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:06.600
I have faith in you.


00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.200
Both of you.


00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:13.200
Where will you find him?


00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:17.900
Tortuga. I'll start there
and I won't stop searching till I find him.


00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:21.600
And then I intend to return here
to marry you.


00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.900
- Properly?
- Eagerly, if you'll still have me.


00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:29.800
If it weren't for these bars,
I'd have you already.


00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:40.300
- I'll wait for you.
- Keep a weather eye on the horizon.


00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.500
Captain Jack Sparrow?


00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:56.700
Owes me four doubloons.
Heard he was dead.


00:19:56.700 --> 00:20:00.500
Singapore. That's what I heard.
Drunk, with a smile on his face.


00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:04.800
Sure as the tide,
Jack Sparrow turn up in Singapore.


00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.700
Jack Sparrow?


00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:12.500
I haven't seen him in a month.
When you find him,
will you give him a message?


00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.900
Can't say about Jack Sparrow,


00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:20.900
but there's an island
just south of the straits
where I trade spice for...


00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.500
mm, delicious long pork.


00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:29.100
Cannot say about Jack,
but you'll find a ship there.


00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.300
A ship with black sails.


00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.800
My brother will take you ashore.


00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.600
- What's wrong? The beach is there.
- <i>C'est trop dangereux.</i>


00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:57.100
- What?
- <i>C'est trop dangereux. je vous l'avais dit.</i>


00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.300
<i>Bon voyage. monsieur.</i>


00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:26.600
Jack!


00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:28.600
Jack Sparrow!


00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.200
Marty!


00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:38.700
Cotton!


00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.500
Anybody?


00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:49.200
- Ah. A familiar face.
-


00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.000
Don't eat me.


00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:54.000
- I'm not going to eat you.
- Don't eat me.


00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.500
No. Don't eat me.


00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.800
Gibbs.


00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.800
Come on! Let's go!


00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.600
Come on! Who wants it?


00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:49.500
I can do this all day!


00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:52.800
Jack?


00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:55.100
Jack Sparrow!


00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:57.800
I can honestly say
I'm glad to see you.


00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:07.900
Jack, it's me.


00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:09.300
Will Turner.


00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:19.100
- Tell them to let me down!
-


00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:21.700
...eensy-weensy.


00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:31.400
...eunuchy. Snip, snip.
-


00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.600
Eunuchy.


00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.600
Jack, the compass.
That's all I need.


00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:44.000
Elizabeth is in danger.
We were arrested for helping you.
She faces the gallows!


00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:53.100
Savvy?


00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.500
Save me.


00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:05.600
Jack, what did you tell them?
No!


00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:08.200
What about Elizabeth?


00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:09.800
Jack!


00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:14.700
That's a good girl.
Come a little closer.


00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:19.000
- We don't bite.
- Go on. We won't bite you.


00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:23.800
Come quickly.


00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:27.100
- Tell me what's happening.
- Our name still has some standing.


00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.400
I've arranged passage to England.
The captain is a friend of mine.


00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:34.500
No! Will's gone to find Jack!
We cannot count on William Turner.


00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:37.600
- Come!
- He's better than you give him credit for.


00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:41.000
This is no time for innocence.
Beckett has offered one pardon only -


00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.100
one, and that is promised
to Jack Sparrow.
Even if Will succeeds,


00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:51.100
do not ask me to endure the sight of my
daughter walking to the gallows. Do not.


00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:55.300
Perhaps I can ensure a fair trial
for Will if he returns.


00:25:55.300 --> 00:26:00.400
- A fair trial for Will ends in a hanging.
- Then there is nothing left for you here.


00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.000
Whoa!


00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:09.200
Wait inside.


00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:12.100
Captain?


00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.400
Captain.


00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:24.200
Evening, Governor.


00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:26.200
Shame, that.


00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.300
He was carrying this.


00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:31.000
It's a letter to the king.


00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.300
- It's from you.
- No...


00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:38.700
Elizabeth.


00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.700
What are you doing?


00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.700
- Where is she?
- Who?


00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.800
No doubt you've discovered that loyalty
is no longer the currency of the realm,


00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:10.000
as your father believes.


00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.300
Then what is?


00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:16.500
I'm afraid currency
is the currency of the realm.


00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:21.800
I expect, then, we can come
to some sort of understanding.


00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:24.900
- I'm here to negotiate.
- I'm listening.


00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:29.400
I'm listening intently.


00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.700
These letters of marque
are signed by the king.


00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:38.400
Yes, and they're not valid
until they bear my signature and my seal.


00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:41.000
Or else I would not still be here.


00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:44.900
You sent Will to get you the compass
owned by Jack Sparrow.


00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:48.600
- It will do you no good.
- Do explain.


00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:53.300
I have been to the Isla de Muerta.
I have seen the treasure myself.


00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:57.300
There is something
you need to know.
Ah, I see.


00:27:57.300 --> 00:28:00.400
You think the compass
leads only to the Isla de Muerta


00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:03.500
and so you hope to save me
from an evil fate.


00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:06.600
But you mustn't worry.


00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:10.400
I care not for cursed Aztec gold.


00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:12.800
My desires are not so provincial.


00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:17.700
There's more than one chest of value
in these waters.


00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:22.700
So perhaps you may wish
to enhance your offer.


00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:29.900
Consider into your calculations
that you robbed me of my wedding night.


00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.900
So I did.


00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:36.000
A marriage interrupted...


00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:40.800
or fate intervenes?


00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:44.700
You're making great efforts
to ensure Jack Sparrow's freedom.


00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:49.900
- These aren't going to Jack.
- Oh, really?
To ensure Mr. Turner's freedom, then,


00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:53.400
I'll still want that compass.


00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:57.000
Consider that in your calculations.


00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:21.400
I say it was Divine Providence
what escaped us from jail.
And I say it was me being clever.


00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:25.000
Ain't that right, poochie?


00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:29.200
Well, how'd you know it weren't Divine
Providence what inspired you to be clever?


00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.200
Anyways, I ain't stealing no ship.


00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:36.500
It ain't stealing. It's salvaging.
And since when did you care?


00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:41.000
Since we're not immortal no more.
We gotta take care of our immortal souls.


00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:44.700
- You know you can't read.
- It's the Bible. You get credit for trying.


00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:50.200
Pretending to read the Bible's a lie.
That's a mark against...


00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:53.500
Look.


00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:55.500
There it is.


00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:01.900
What's got into him?


00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:04.000
Must have seen a catfish.


00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.000
Stupid mongrel!


00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:21.400
Argh!


00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:29.800
Come on!


00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:41.100
- It's ours for the taking.
- Tide's coming in. That should help.


00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:45.600
And salvaging is saving,
in a manner of speaking.
There's the truth of it!


00:30:52.900 --> 00:30:55.900
Suppose we better save it
as soon as we can,


00:30:55.700 --> 00:31:01.600
what with our souls
in such a vulnerable state and all.
Amen to that.


00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:19.000
Thank you.


00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.500
Why would he do this to us?
If Jack is the chief...


00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:40.800
Aye, the Pelegostos
made Jack their chief,


00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:44.400
but he only remains chief
as long as he acts like a chief.


00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:47.500
So he had no choice.
He's a captive as much as the rest of us.


00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:54.100
Worse, as it turns out. See, the Pelegostos
believe that Jack is a god in human form,


00:31:53.900 --> 00:32:00.000
and they intend to do him the honor
of releasing him from his fleshy prison.


00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.700
They'll roast him and eat him.


00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:12.900
Where's the rest of the crew?
These cages we're in
weren't built till after we got here.


00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.200
The feast is about to begin.


00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:19.800
Jack's life will end


00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:22.100
when the drums stop.


00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:27.200
Well, we can't just sit here and wait,
then, can we?


00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:31.300
No, no. Oi! No, no.


00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:34.400
More wood. Big fire. Big fire.


00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:36.400
I am Chief. Want big fire.


00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:38.500
Come on.


00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.900
Oi.
<i>Tout de suite.</i>


00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:44.200
Come on. More wood.


00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:11.900
Whoa!


00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:51.700
Oh, bugger.


00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:03.100
A little seasoning, eh?


00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:17.900
Well done.


00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:58.900
Put your legs through.
Start to climb!


00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:04.000
Come on, men!
It'll take all of us to crew the <i>Black Pearl!</i>


00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:06.600
Actually, you wouldn't need everyone.


00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:08.900
About six would do.


00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:14.100
Oh, dear.


00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:20.000
- Hurry!
- Come on!


00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:25.900
Come on! Is that all you got?


00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:35.600
- Wait. Stop.
- Eh?


00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:38.400
Stop. Shh.


00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:47.200
Shh.


00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.300
- What are they doing?
- Stop.


00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:01.100
- Snake!
-


00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.800
Move!


00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:47.200
Well, go on, go get them.


00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:53.600
No, no. Oi! No, no!


00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:57.400
Not good.


00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:15.100
Cut it loose! Find a rock!


00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.100
Roll the cage!


00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:37.400
Come on!


00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:05.800
- Lift the cage! Hurry!
- Come on, lads. Lift it like a lady's skirt.


00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:00.900
Run!


00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.800
This way, lads!


00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:39.700
Take cover!


00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.300
Stop it!


00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:36.600
Bugger.


00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:05.600
Haul loose the mooring line!


00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:07.900
The mooring line!


00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:11.500
Thief! Little hairy thief!
Give it back!


00:41:11.500 --> 00:41:14.800
- Don't bite it!
- Haul loose the mooring line!


00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:17.300
He's got me eye.
He won't give it back.


00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:20.700
- How'd you get it back last time?
- Excellent! Our work's half done.


00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:23.200
We done it for you,
knowing you'd be coming back.


00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:26.100
- Make ready to sail, boys!
- What about Jack?


00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:29.300
- I won't leave without him.
- Oi!


00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:40.000
- Time to go.
- Cast off those lines!


00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:48.000
Aaargh!


00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:51.300
- Make ready to cast off!
- Oi!


00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:56.900
Good doggy.


00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:17.900
Alas, my children, this is the day you shall
always remember as the day you almost...


00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:22.900
Captain Jack Sparrow.


00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:50.600
Let's get away from this island
and head out to open sea.


00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:55.000
Yes to the first. Yes to the second, but
only insofar as we keep to the shallows.


00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.000
That seems a bit contradictory, Captain.


00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:00.100
I have faith in your reconciliatory
navigational skills.


00:43:00.100 --> 00:43:03.500
Now, where is that monkey?
I want to shoot something.


00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.000
Jack. Elizabeth is in danger.


00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:13.800
Have you considered
just locking her up somewhere?


00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:16.200
She is locked up,
bound to hang for helping you.


00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:21.200
There comes a time when one must
take responsibility for one's mistakes.


00:43:22.200 --> 00:43:27.200
I need that compass of yours, Jack.
I must trade it for her freedom.


00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:32.000
- Mr. Gibbs.
- Captain.


00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:35.000
We have a need to travel upriver.


00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:38.400
By need, do you mean a trifling need?


00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:42.200
Fleeting?
As in, say, a passing fancy?


00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:45.900
No, a resolute and unyielding need.


00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:51.500
What we need to do
is make sail for Port Royal with all haste.
William,


00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:55.300
I shall trade you the compass
if you will help me


00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:57.500
to find this.


00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:03.400
- You want me to find this?
- No.


00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:06.900
You want you to find this,


00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:11.000
because the finding of this
finds you incapacitorially finding


00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:17.600
and/or locating in your discovering a way
to save your dolly belle, ol' what's-her-face.


00:44:17.300 --> 00:44:20.200
Savvy?


00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:24.100
This is going to save Elizabeth?


00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:29.200
How much do you know
about Davy Jones?


00:44:31.300 --> 00:44:32.900
Not much.


00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:35.900
Yeah, it's going to save Elizabeth.


00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:03.700
What's all this?


00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:09.100
If you both fancy the dress, you'll just have
to share and wear it one after the other.


00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:12.300
It's not like that, sir.
This ship is haunted.


00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:15.100
Is it, now? And you?


00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:19.200
There's a female presence
amongst us here, sir.


00:45:19.100 --> 00:45:21.600
All the men, they can feel it.


00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:25.300
It's the ghost of a lady widowed
before her marriage, I figure it,


00:45:25.200 --> 00:45:30.000
searching for her husband,
lost at sea.
A virgin too, likely as not.


00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.300
And that bodes ill by all accounts.


00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:36.800
I say that we throw the dress overboard
and we hope the spirit follows it.


00:45:36.700 --> 00:45:39.500
No! That will just anger the spirit, sir.


00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:43.600
What we need to do is find out what
the spirit needs, and then get it back to her.


00:45:43.400 --> 00:45:45.700
Enough! Enough!


00:45:45.800 --> 00:45:50.100
You're a pair of superstitious goats
and it's got the best of you.


00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:53.200
Now, this appears to be no more
as we have a stowaway on board.


00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:57.800
A young woman, by the look of it.
I want you to search the ship
and find her.


00:45:57.800 --> 00:46:00.500
Oh, and, er... she's probably naked.


00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:31.800
Why is Jack afraid
of the open ocean?


00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.100
Well, if you believe such things,


00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:37.300
there's a beast does the bidding
of Davy Jones -


00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:41.900
a fearsome creature with giant tentacles
that'll suction your face clean off


00:46:41.800 --> 00:46:45.400
and drag an entire ship
down to the crushing darkness.


00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:48.900
The kraken.


00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:55.000
They say the stench of its breath is like...


00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:58.400
Imagine, the last thing you know
on God's green earth


00:46:58.300 --> 00:47:04.300
is the roar of the kraken and the reeking
odor of a thousand rotting corpses.


00:47:05.800 --> 00:47:08.300
If you believe such things.


00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:11.100
And the key will spare him that?


00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:14.600
Well, that's the very question
Jack wants answered.


00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:17.100
Bad enough even to go visit...


00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:20.000
her.


00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.800
Her?


00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:23.800
Aye.


00:47:57.300 --> 00:48:01.000
No worries, mates.
Tia Dalma and I go way back.


00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:05.000
Thick as thieves.
Nigh inseparable, we are.


00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:07.200
Were. Have been.


00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:12.200
Before.
- I'll watch your back.
- It's me front I'm worried about.


00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:14.700
- Mind the boat.
- Mind the boat.


00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:18.000
- Mind the boat.
- Mind the boat.


00:48:18.000 --> 00:48:20.100
- Mind the boat.
-


00:48:20.100 --> 00:48:22.200
Mind the boat.


00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:36.700
Jack Sparrow.


00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:38.800
Tia Dalma.


00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:44.000
I always knew the wind was going
to blow you back to me one day.


00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:50.300
You...


00:48:52.600 --> 00:48:55.200
You have a touch of...


00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:58.500
destiny about you,


00:48:58.400 --> 00:49:00.300
William Turner.


00:49:01.900 --> 00:49:04.200
You know me?


00:49:04.100 --> 00:49:06.800
You want to know me.


00:49:06.700 --> 00:49:11.400
There'll be no knowing here. We've come
for help and we're not leaving without it.


00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:14.900
- I thought I knew you.
- Not so well as I had hoped.


00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:17.000
- Come.
- Come.


00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:24.900
What service may I do you?


00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:29.400
- You know I demand payment.
- I brought payment.


00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:34.700
Look.


00:49:35.700 --> 00:49:38.100
An undead monkey.


00:49:38.100 --> 00:49:40.300
Top that.


00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:47.900
Don't! You've no idea
how long it took us to catch that.


00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:50.700
The payment is fair.


00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:52.700
We're looking for this.


00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.800
And what it goes to.


00:49:59.100 --> 00:50:03.800
The compass you bartered from me,
it cannot lead you to this?


00:50:03.700 --> 00:50:07.100
Maybe. Why?


00:50:07.100 --> 00:50:09.400
I hear you.


00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:13.800
Jack Sparrow does not know
what he wants.


00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:16.500
Or do you know,


00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:20.400
but are loath to claim it as your own?


00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:24.000
Your key go to a chest.


00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:29.100
And it is what lay inside the chest
you seek, don't it?


00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:31.900
- What is inside?
- Gold?


00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:35.900
Jewels? Unclaimed properties
of a valuable nature?


00:50:35.800 --> 00:50:39.000
Nothing bad, I hope?


00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:42.400
You know of Davy Jones,


00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:43.900
yes?


00:50:43.900 --> 00:50:46.700
A man of the sea.


00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:49.000
A great sailor,


00:50:48.900 --> 00:50:54.000
until he run afoul of that
which vex all men.


00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:56.800
What vexes all men?


00:50:58.200 --> 00:51:00.700
What indeed?


00:51:01.100 --> 00:51:03.200
- The sea.
- Sums.


00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:06.200
The dichotomy of good and evil.


00:51:06.700 --> 00:51:08.400
A woman.


00:51:08.600 --> 00:51:10.200
A woman.


00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:12.300
He fell in love.


00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:15.200
No, no, no, no.
I heard it was the sea he fell in love with.


00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:19.200
Same story, different versions,
and all are true.


00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:21.400
See, it was a woman


00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:24.200
as changing and harsh


00:51:24.100 --> 00:51:27.500
and untamable as the sea.


00:51:27.500 --> 00:51:30.000
Him never stopped loving her.


00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:34.700
But the pain it cause him
was too much to live with


00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:37.300
but not enough to cause him to die.


00:51:38.900 --> 00:51:43.400
What exactly did he put
into the chest?


00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:45.200
Him heart.


00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:47.300
Literally or figuratively?


00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:51.800
He couldn't literally
put his heart in a chest.


00:51:51.700 --> 00:51:53.700
Could he?


00:51:53.700 --> 00:51:59.800
It was not worth feeling
what small, fleeting joy life brings.


00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:01.300
And so...


00:52:01.300 --> 00:52:04.000
him carve out him heart,


00:52:04.500 --> 00:52:09.200
lock it away in a chest
and hide the chest from the world.


00:52:09.900 --> 00:52:12.000
The key


00:52:11.900 --> 00:52:16.500
he keep with him at all times.


00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:20.600
- You knew this.
- I did not. I didn't know where the key was.


00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:24.700
But now we do. All that's left is to climb
aboard the <i>Flying Dutchman,</i> grab the key,


00:52:24.600 --> 00:52:30.200
you go back to Port Royal
and save your bonnie lass, eh?
Let me see your hand.


00:52:43.100 --> 00:52:45.600
The black spot!


00:52:46.500 --> 00:52:50.000
- The black spot!
- Black spot!


00:52:50.300 --> 00:52:53.400
My eyesight's as good as ever,
just so you know.


00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.800
I have just the thing.
Now, where did I put it?


00:52:59.900 --> 00:53:03.500
My little beauty, where are you?


00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:07.000
Such a long time in such a mess.


00:53:08.800 --> 00:53:11.600
Davy Jones cannot make port.


00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:14.900
Cannot step on land
but once every ten years.


00:53:15.400 --> 00:53:18.600
Land is where you are safe,
Jack Sparrow,


00:53:18.500 --> 00:53:21.100
and so you will carry land with you.


00:53:29.800 --> 00:53:31.800
Dirt.


00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:36.500
- This is a jar of dirt.
- Yes.


00:53:38.600 --> 00:53:41.500
Is the jar of dirt going to help?


00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:44.400
If you don't want it, give it back.


00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:47.900
- No.
- Then it helps.


00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:55.000
It seems we have a need to find
the <i>Flying Dutchman.</i>


00:53:57.800 --> 00:54:01.600
A touch of destiny.


00:54:16.300 --> 00:54:18.800
That's the <i>Flying Dutchman?</i>


00:54:20.500 --> 00:54:25.700
- She doesn't look like much.
- Neither do you.
Do not underestimate her.


00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:31.400
- Must have run afoul of the reef.
- So, what's your plan, then?


00:54:31.200 --> 00:54:37.100
I row over, search the ship
until I find your bloody key.
And if there are crewmen?


00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:39.300
I cut down anyone in my path.


00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:43.000
I like it. Simple, easy to remember.


00:54:42.800 --> 00:54:47.000
Your chariot awaits you, Sire.


00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:50.100
Oi!


00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:55.800
If you do happen to get captured, just say
Jack Sparrow sent you to settle his debt.


00:54:55.700 --> 00:54:59.600
- It might save your life.
- Bon voyage!


00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:05.900
Douse the lamps.


00:55:45.600 --> 00:55:46.700
Sailor.


00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:49.400
- Hoisting the jib, Captain's orders.
- Sailor!


00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:51.800
Hoisting the jib.
Bring up with a round turn.


00:55:51.700 --> 00:55:54.500
There's no use.
You've run aground.


00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:55.800
No.


00:55:55.800 --> 00:55:57.900
Beneath us.


00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:01.100
Foul breath.


00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:15.600
Hey! Hey!


00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:54.200
Down on your marrowbones and pray.


00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:12.400
Get back! Get back!


00:57:42.500 --> 00:57:46.200
Five men still alive.
The rest have moved on.


00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:05.600
Do you fear death?


00:58:06.800 --> 00:58:11.700
Do you fear that dark abyss?


00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:16.200
All your deeds laid bare.


00:58:16.900 --> 00:58:19.900
All your sins punished.


00:58:21.500 --> 00:58:24.000
I can offer you


00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:26.500
an escape.


00:58:26.500 --> 00:58:29.500
Don't listen to him!


00:58:37.700 --> 00:58:40.700
Do you not fear death?


00:58:42.900 --> 00:58:45.500
I'll take my chances, sir.


00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:47.900
To the depths.


00:58:53.100 --> 00:58:55.100
Cruel blackguard!


00:58:55.100 --> 00:58:57.200
Life is cruel.


00:58:58.000 --> 00:59:00.900
Why should the afterlife
be any different?


00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:04.400
I offer you a choice.


00:59:04.400 --> 00:59:07.100
Join my crew


00:59:07.100 --> 00:59:09.400
and postpone the judgment.


00:59:10.600 --> 00:59:14.100
One hundred years before the mast.


00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:18.100
Will ye serve?


00:59:19.700 --> 00:59:21.700
I-I will serve.


00:59:21.700 --> 00:59:24.200
There.


00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:34.100
You are neither dead nor dying.


00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:36.700
What is your purpose here?


00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:42.500
Jack Sparrow sent me
to settle his debt.


00:59:43.400 --> 00:59:46.700
<i>What</i> is your purpose here?


00:59:46.700 --> 00:59:49.900
Jack Sparrow
sent me to settle his debt.


00:59:52.500 --> 00:59:55.100
Did he, now?


00:59:55.100 --> 00:59:58.600
I'm sorely tempted to accept that offer.


01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:14.200
Oh.


01:00:14.700 --> 01:00:17.500
You have a debt to pay.


01:00:17.500 --> 01:00:22.900
You've been captain of the <i>Black Pearl</i>
for 13 years.
That was our agreement.


01:00:22.900 --> 01:00:26.700
Technically I was only captain
for two years, then I was mutinied upon.


01:00:26.700 --> 01:00:30.500
Then you were a poor captain,
but a captain nonetheless.


01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:33.600
Have you not introduced yourself
all these years


01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:36.200
as Captain Jack Sparrow?


01:00:38.800 --> 01:00:41.800
You have my payment.
One soul to serve on your ship.


01:00:41.800 --> 01:00:45.100
- One soul is not equal to another.
- Aha!


01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:51.600
We've established my proposal is sound
in principle. Now we're haggling over price.


01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:53.900
Price?


01:00:55.700 --> 01:00:59.200
Just how many souls
do you think my soul is worth?


01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:05.400
One hundred souls.


01:01:06.200 --> 01:01:08.000
Three days.


01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:12.300
You're a diamond, mate. Send me back
the boy. I'll get started right off.


01:01:12.200 --> 01:01:15.300
I keep the boy.
A good-faith payment.


01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:19.300
That leaves you only 99 more to go.


01:01:20.700 --> 01:01:25.600
Have you not met Will Turner?
He's noble, heroic,
a terrific soprano.


01:01:25.500 --> 01:01:27.800
Worth at least four.


01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:30.100
Maybe three and a half.


01:01:30.800 --> 01:01:33.800
And did I happen to mention...


01:01:33.700 --> 01:01:36.200
he's in love?


01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:39.600
With a girl.


01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:41.900
Due to be married.


01:01:41.900 --> 01:01:43.800
Betrothed.


01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:49.400
Dividing him from her and her from him
would only be half as cruel


01:01:49.300 --> 01:01:53.300
as actually allowing them
to be joined in holy matrimony.


01:01:53.300 --> 01:01:55.300
Eh?


01:01:57.800 --> 01:02:02.700
I keep the boy. 99 souls.


01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:05.300
But I wonder, Sparrow,


01:02:05.200 --> 01:02:07.500
can you live with this?


01:02:08.000 --> 01:02:11.900
Can you condemn an innocent man,
a friend,


01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:16.800
to a lifetime of servitude in your name
while you roam free?


01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:21.600
Yep. I'm good with it.


01:02:21.700 --> 01:02:24.900
Shall we seal it in blood?
I mean... ink.


01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:31.500
Three days.


01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:37.900
- Three days.
-


01:02:45.100 --> 01:02:48.100
- Er, Mr. Gibbs.
- Aye.


01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:50.700
I-I feel sullied and unusual.


01:02:50.600 --> 01:02:56.400
And how do you intend to harvest
these 99 souls in three days?


01:02:56.300 --> 01:03:01.000
Fortunately, he was mum as to
the condition in which these souls need be.


01:03:00.900 --> 01:03:02.100
Ah.


01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:04.100
Tortuga.


01:03:05.100 --> 01:03:06.600
Tortuga.


01:03:10.300 --> 01:03:14.000
It's an outrage.
Port tariffs, berthing fees,


01:03:13.900 --> 01:03:17.100
wharf handling
and, heaven help us, pilotage.


01:03:17.100 --> 01:03:21.500
Are we all to work for
the East India Trading Company, then?


01:03:21.400 --> 01:03:25.800
I'm afraid, sir, Tortuga is
the only free port left in these waters.


01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:28.300
A pirate port is what you mean.
Well, I'm sorry.


01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:33.800
An honest sailor is what I am. I make
my living fair and I sleep well each night.


01:03:34.300 --> 01:03:36.200
S-sir!


01:03:58.400 --> 01:04:00.900
She wants you
to do something.


01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:06.500
She's trying to give a sign.


01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:11.800
Whoa!


01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:16.900
Over there! Look for a sign!


01:04:21.400 --> 01:04:23.600
Look, there! There it is!


01:04:23.900 --> 01:04:26.500
- There's the sign!
- That's seaweed.


01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:29.600
- Seaweed can be a sign.
- Looks like entrails.


01:04:29.500 --> 01:04:34.600
- That would be a bad sign.
- What's that over there?


01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:55.200
And what makes you think
you're worthy to crew the <i>Black Pearl?</i>


01:04:55.100 --> 01:04:57.400
I've never sailed a day in me life.


01:04:57.300 --> 01:05:00.500
I figure I should get out,
see the world while I'm still young.


01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:03.900
You'll do. Make your mark.
Next!


01:05:03.800 --> 01:05:06.500
My wife ran off with my dog


01:05:06.800 --> 01:05:09.300
and I'm drunk for a month


01:05:09.300 --> 01:05:13.300
and I don't give an ass rat's
if I live or die.


01:05:13.300 --> 01:05:16.300
- Perfect. Next!
- I know what I want. I know what I want...


01:05:16.300 --> 01:05:20.400
- Me have one arm and a bum leg.
- It's the crow's nest for you.


01:05:20.300 --> 01:05:22.500
- I know what I want.
- Next!


01:05:22.500 --> 01:05:28.100
Ever since I was a little lad, I've always
wanted to sail the seas... forever.


01:05:28.000 --> 01:05:30.700
Sooner than you think.
Sign the roster.


01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:33.000
Thanks very much.


01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:38.100
- How we going?
- Including those four, that gives us... four.


01:05:41.100 --> 01:05:43.400
And what's your story?


01:05:43.300 --> 01:05:45.800
My story.


01:05:45.800 --> 01:05:50.900
It's exactly the same as your story,
just one chapter behind.


01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:54.400
I chased a man
across the seven seas.


01:05:54.400 --> 01:05:57.300
The pursuit cost me my crew,


01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:59.900
my commission,


01:05:59.800 --> 01:06:01.900
and my life.


01:06:06.700 --> 01:06:11.500
- Commodore?
- No, not anymore. Weren't you listening?


01:06:14.200 --> 01:06:16.800
I nearly had you all off Tripoli.


01:06:17.400 --> 01:06:22.000
I would have,
if not for the hurricane.


01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:23.300
Lord.


01:06:23.300 --> 01:06:27.900
You didn't try to sail through it?
So, do I make your crew


01:06:27.800 --> 01:06:29.800
or not?


01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:33.300
You haven't said where you're going.


01:06:33.300 --> 01:06:35.300
Somewhere nice?!


01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:38.700
So, am I worthy to serve


01:06:38.700 --> 01:06:41.500
under Captain Jack Sparrow?


01:06:43.700 --> 01:06:47.100
Or should I just kill you now?


01:06:52.800 --> 01:06:55.000
You're hired.


01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:59.000
Sorry. Old habits and all that.


01:06:58.900 --> 01:07:02.500
- Easy, sailor!
- That's our captain you're threatening.


01:07:14.900 --> 01:07:16.900
- Time to go.
- Aye!


01:07:59.700 --> 01:08:02.700
- Thanks, mate.
- Aargh!


01:08:07.100 --> 01:08:09.000
Carry on.


01:08:15.900 --> 01:08:18.500
Come on, then! Who wants some?


01:08:18.400 --> 01:08:22.000
Form an orderly line
and I'll have you all, one by one.


01:08:21.900 --> 01:08:24.300
Come on, who's first?


01:08:26.700 --> 01:08:30.200
I just wanted the pleasure
of doing that myself.


01:08:55.600 --> 01:08:58.300
James Norrington.


01:08:59.000 --> 01:09:02.100
What has the world done to you?


01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:31.200
Heave!


01:09:31.400 --> 01:09:33.600
Heave!


01:09:34.200 --> 01:09:36.200
Heave!


01:09:36.300 --> 01:09:38.200
Heave!


01:09:39.200 --> 01:09:42.600
- Heave!
- Heave!


01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:49.300
Secure the mast tackle, Mr. Turner!


01:09:52.000 --> 01:09:54.100
Set to it!


01:09:59.100 --> 01:10:02.000
- Step aside.
- Hey! Mind yourself!


01:10:02.300 --> 01:10:04.700
Let go, boy!


01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:09.300
No.


01:10:16.900 --> 01:10:19.600
Haul that weevil to his feet.


01:10:24.800 --> 01:10:28.200
Five lashes will remind you
to stay on 'em!


01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:33.300
- No!
- Impeding me in my duties?


01:10:33.200 --> 01:10:37.000
- You'll share the punishment.
- I'll take it all.


01:10:36.900 --> 01:10:39.300
Will you, now?


01:10:39.300 --> 01:10:44.500
And what would prompt
such an act of charity?


01:10:47.800 --> 01:10:49.800
My son.


01:10:54.500 --> 01:10:56.800
He's my son.


01:11:07.400 --> 01:11:10.700
What fortuitous circumstance be this?


01:11:10.700 --> 01:11:14.600
Five lashes be owed, I believe it is.


01:11:19.100 --> 01:11:21.600
No. No, I won't!


01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:24.400
The cat's out of the bag, Mr. Turner.


01:11:24.700 --> 01:11:30.200
Your issue will feel its sting,
be it by the bosun's hand or your own.


01:11:33.600 --> 01:11:36.000
No.


01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:38.000
- Bosun!
- No!


01:12:20.300 --> 01:12:23.700
You had it easy, boy!


01:12:23.700 --> 01:12:26.700
- Will.
- I don't need your help.


01:12:28.200 --> 01:12:34.600
The bosun prides himself on cleaving flesh
from bone, with every swing.


01:12:34.300 --> 01:12:39.300
So I'm to understand
what you did was an act of compassion?


01:12:39.200 --> 01:12:41.300
Yes.


01:12:47.000 --> 01:12:52.900
100 years before the mast,
losing who you were, bit by bit,


01:12:52.600 --> 01:12:56.500
until you end up...
end up like poor Wyvern here.


01:12:58.100 --> 01:13:02.200
Once you've sworn an oath
to the <i>Dutchman,</i> there's no leaving it.


01:13:02.000 --> 01:13:05.200
Not until your debt is paid.


01:13:05.100 --> 01:13:07.600
I've sworn no oath.


01:13:09.100 --> 01:13:11.300
You must get away.


01:13:12.200 --> 01:13:14.300
Not until I find this.


01:13:15.800 --> 01:13:17.700
The key.


01:13:24.600 --> 01:13:27.000
The dead man's chest.


01:13:28.000 --> 01:13:30.100
What do you know of this?


01:13:30.100 --> 01:13:34.800
Open the chest with the key,
and stab the heart...


01:13:34.700 --> 01:13:37.800
No, don't stab the heart.


01:13:37.800 --> 01:13:42.900
The <i>Dutchman</i> needs a living heart
or there'll be no captain,


01:13:42.700 --> 01:13:47.000
and if there's no captain,
there's no one to have the key.


01:13:47.200 --> 01:13:49.800
So the captain has the key?


01:13:50.900 --> 01:13:53.600
- Where is the key?
- Hidden.


01:13:53.900 --> 01:13:56.700
- Where is the chest?
- Hidden.


01:13:59.700 --> 01:14:01.500
Captain Sparrow!


01:14:01.500 --> 01:14:06.600
Come to join me crew, lad?
Welcome aboard.
I'm here to find the man I love.


01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:11.900
I'm deeply flattered, son,
but my first and only love is the sea.


01:14:12.800 --> 01:14:17.000
Meaning William Turner,
Captain Sparrow.


01:14:16.900 --> 01:14:18.200
Elizabeth.


01:14:18.300 --> 01:14:20.700
Hide the rum.


01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:23.600
These clothes do not flatter you.
It should be a dress or nothing.


01:14:23.500 --> 01:14:26.700
- I happen to have no dress in my cabin.
- Jack.


01:14:28.600 --> 01:14:31.900
I know Will came to find you.
Where is he?


01:14:31.800 --> 01:14:35.400
Darling, I am truly unhappy
to have to tell you this, but...


01:14:35.300 --> 01:14:42.200
through unfortunate circumstances
that had nothing whatsoever to do with me,


01:14:42.100 --> 01:14:46.800
poor Will has been press-ganged
into Davy Jones's crew.


01:14:47.500 --> 01:14:49.400
Davy Jones?


01:14:50.800 --> 01:14:53.100
Oh, please.


01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:56.500
The captain of the <i>Flying Dutchman?</i>


01:14:56.500 --> 01:14:59.900
- You look awful. What are you doing here?
- You hired me.


01:14:59.900 --> 01:15:05.000
I can't help it if your standards are lax.
- You smell funny.
- Jack!


01:15:05.300 --> 01:15:08.100
All I want is to find Will.


01:15:11.200 --> 01:15:15.600
Are you certain?
Is that what you really want most?


01:15:16.400 --> 01:15:18.700
Of course.


01:15:18.700 --> 01:15:23.000
Because I would think you'd want to find
a way to save Will most.


01:15:23.400 --> 01:15:27.300
- And you'd have a way of doing that?
- Well...


01:15:27.200 --> 01:15:30.400
- there is a chest.
- Oh, dear.


01:15:30.400 --> 01:15:37.600
A chest of unknown size and origin.
What contains
the still-beating heart of Davy Jones.


01:15:38.700 --> 01:15:40.600
And whoever possesses that chest


01:15:40.700 --> 01:15:44.700
possesses the leverage to command Jones
to do whatever it is he or she wants,


01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:49.500
including saving brave William
from his grim fate.


01:15:49.300 --> 01:15:52.900
You don't actually believe him, do you?


01:15:57.200 --> 01:15:59.600
How would we find it?


01:16:01.700 --> 01:16:03.500
With this.


01:16:03.500 --> 01:16:06.400
My compass... is unique.


01:16:06.700 --> 01:16:10.000
"Unique" here
having the meaning of "broken."


01:16:10.900 --> 01:16:15.300
True enough,
this compass does not point north.


01:16:17.200 --> 01:16:20.000
Where does it point?


01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:24.800
It points to the thing
you want most in this world.


01:16:27.200 --> 01:16:29.200
Oh, Jack.


01:16:31.000 --> 01:16:34.700
- Are you telling the truth?
- Every word, love.


01:16:34.600 --> 01:16:37.200
And what you want most in this world


01:16:37.900 --> 01:16:42.800
is to find the chest of Davy Jones,
is it not?


01:16:42.700 --> 01:16:47.600
- To save Will.
- By finding the chest of Davy Jones.


01:17:02.600 --> 01:17:04.900
- Mr. Gibbs!
- Cap'n.


01:17:04.900 --> 01:17:07.200
- We have our heading.
- Finally!


01:17:07.100 --> 01:17:11.700
Cast off those lines.
Weigh anchor and crowd that canvas!


01:17:11.600 --> 01:17:14.900
- Miss Swann.
-


01:17:15.700 --> 01:17:19.100
Welcome to the crew,
former commodore.


01:17:25.300 --> 01:17:30.200
There's something to knowing the exact
shape of the world and one's place in it.


01:17:30.100 --> 01:17:32.800
I assure you, these are not necessary.


01:17:32.700 --> 01:17:37.200
I thought you'd be interested
in the whereabouts of your daughter.


01:17:37.100 --> 01:17:40.600
- You have news of her?
- Most recently seen on Tortuga,


01:17:40.500 --> 01:17:44.500
and then left in the company
of a known pirate, Jack Sparrow,


01:17:44.400 --> 01:17:47.400
and other fugitives from justice.


01:17:47.300 --> 01:17:49.100
"Justice"? Hardly.


01:17:49.200 --> 01:17:55.000
Including the previous owner of this sword,
I believe.


01:17:55.200 --> 01:17:59.900
Our ships are in pursuit. Justice will
be dispensed by cannonade and cutlass,


01:17:59.800 --> 01:18:03.800
and all manner
of remorseless pieces of metal.


01:18:03.600 --> 01:18:09.600
I find it distasteful to even contemplate
the horror facing all those on board.


01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:17.600
What do you want from me?
Your authority as governor,
your influence in London,


01:18:17.600 --> 01:18:21.200
and your loyalty
to the East India Trading Company.


01:18:22.000 --> 01:18:24.600
To you, you mean.


01:18:27.900 --> 01:18:30.700
Shall I remove these shackles?


01:18:33.700 --> 01:18:37.100
Do what you can for my daughter.


01:18:43.000 --> 01:18:47.900
So you see, Mercer, every man
has a price he will willingly accept.


01:18:47.800 --> 01:18:51.600
Even for what he hoped never to sell.


01:18:57.000 --> 01:19:00.100
I wager... ten years.


01:19:00.700 --> 01:19:03.100
I'll match ten years.


01:19:04.000 --> 01:19:05.700
Agreed.


01:19:08.500 --> 01:19:11.300
Wondering how it's played?


01:19:11.200 --> 01:19:13.100
- I understand.
- Liar!


01:19:13.100 --> 01:19:18.700
It's a game of deception. But your bet
includes all the dice, not just your own.


01:19:18.800 --> 01:19:24.100
- What are they wagering?
- Oh, the only thing we have.
Years of service.


01:19:26.100 --> 01:19:30.300
- So any crew member can be challenged?
- Aye. Anyone.


01:19:32.900 --> 01:19:35.600
I challenge Davy Jones.


01:19:54.400 --> 01:19:57.400
I accept, mate.


01:20:08.300 --> 01:20:13.900
- The stakes?
- My soul. An eternity of servitude.


01:20:13.700 --> 01:20:15.900
No!


01:20:17.900 --> 01:20:19.400
Against?


01:20:20.800 --> 01:20:22.800
I want this.


01:20:34.400 --> 01:20:39.300
- How do you know of the key?
- That's not part of the game, is it?


01:20:42.200 --> 01:20:44.800
You can still walk away.


01:21:08.400 --> 01:21:14.300
- What's this?
- I'm in. Matching his wager.
No! Don't do this.


01:21:16.000 --> 01:21:18.800
The die is cast.


01:21:18.700 --> 01:21:21.800
I bid three twos.


01:21:21.800 --> 01:21:24.000
It's your bid, Captain.


01:21:29.900 --> 01:21:32.900
Four fours.


01:21:41.600 --> 01:21:44.800
Four fives.


01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:46.300
Six threes.


01:21:55.600 --> 01:21:58.700
Seven fives.


01:22:21.400 --> 01:22:23.800
Eight fives.


01:22:28.300 --> 01:22:31.800
Welcome to the crew, lad.


01:22:33.700 --> 01:22:36.600
Twelve fives.


01:22:36.800 --> 01:22:38.500
Twelve fives.


01:22:38.500 --> 01:22:41.300
Call me a liar, or up the bid.


01:22:42.500 --> 01:22:46.100
And be called a liar myself
for my trouble?


01:22:49.200 --> 01:22:54.100
Bootstrap Bill, you're a liar
and you will spend an eternity on this ship.


01:22:55.700 --> 01:22:59.900
Master Turner,
feel free to go ashore...


01:23:01.000 --> 01:23:04.200
the very next time we make port!


01:23:11.300 --> 01:23:14.800
Fool. Why did you do that?


01:23:16.200 --> 01:23:18.400
I couldn't let you lose.


01:23:18.400 --> 01:23:21.100
It was never about winning or losing.


01:23:23.300 --> 01:23:24.700
The key.


01:23:26.300 --> 01:23:29.200
You just wanted to know where it was.


01:23:47.900 --> 01:23:50.500
Captain says I'm to relieve you.


01:23:54.200 --> 01:23:56.300
Captain's orders.


01:25:41.500 --> 01:25:44.100
Here. Take this, too.


01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:48.400
Now, get yourself to land, and stay there.


01:25:48.600 --> 01:25:51.900
It was always in my blood to die at sea.


01:25:51.900 --> 01:25:54.900
It was not a fate I ever wanted for you.


01:25:59.300 --> 01:26:03.000
It's not a fate you had to choose
for yourself, either.


01:26:02.900 --> 01:26:09.100
Aye. I could say I did what I had to,
when I left you to go pirating.


01:26:09.800 --> 01:26:14.100
But it would taste a lie
to say it wasn't what I wanted.


01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:18.300
You owe me nothing, Will.


01:26:18.200 --> 01:26:20.300
Now go.


01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:24.100
- They'll know you helped me.
-


01:26:25.300 --> 01:26:27.500
What more can they do to me?


01:26:32.800 --> 01:26:35.100
I take this with a promise.


01:26:35.000 --> 01:26:37.500
I'll find a way to sever
Jones's hold on you,


01:26:37.400 --> 01:26:41.300
and not rest until this blade
pierces his heart.


01:26:42.600 --> 01:26:44.900
I will not abandon you.


01:26:47.400 --> 01:26:49.900
I promise.


01:27:03.400 --> 01:27:05.600
- Beckett?
- Yes, they're signed.


01:27:05.500 --> 01:27:09.500
Lord Cutler Beckett
of the East India Trading Company.
Bluergh!


01:27:09.600 --> 01:27:13.300
Will was working for Beckett
and never said a word.


01:27:13.500 --> 01:27:17.300
Beckett wants the compass.
Only one reason for that.


01:27:17.100 --> 01:27:19.700
Of course.


01:27:19.700 --> 01:27:23.400
- He wants the chest.
- Yes, he did say something about a chest.


01:27:23.400 --> 01:27:27.000
If the company controls the chest,
they controls the sea.


01:27:26.900 --> 01:27:30.700
- A truly discomfiting notion, love.
- And bad.


01:27:30.700 --> 01:27:34.300
Bad for every mother's son
what calls himself a pirate.


01:27:34.200 --> 01:27:39.600
I think there's a bit more speed
to be coaxed from these sails.
Brace the foreyard!


01:27:39.600 --> 01:27:42.800
Might I enquire
as to how you came by these?


01:27:42.700 --> 01:27:44.700
- Persuasion.
- Friendly?


01:27:44.700 --> 01:27:46.300
Decidedly not.


01:27:46.300 --> 01:27:51.900
Will strikes a deal, yet you were the one
with the prize - full pardon.


01:27:51.700 --> 01:27:56.800
"Commission as a privateer
on behalf of England
"and the East India Trading Company."


01:27:56.700 --> 01:27:59.000
As if I could be bought
for such a low price.


01:27:59.000 --> 01:28:03.800
- Jack, the letters, give them back.
- No.
Persuade me.


01:28:03.800 --> 01:28:07.500
You do know Will taught me
how to handle a sword?


01:28:09.700 --> 01:28:13.400
As I said, persuade me.


01:28:27.000 --> 01:28:29.500
It's a curious thing.


01:28:30.400 --> 01:28:36.000
There was a time I would have wanted you
to look like that while thinking about me.


01:28:35.900 --> 01:28:38.100
- I don't know what you mean.
- I think you do.


01:28:38.100 --> 01:28:41.100
Oh, don't be absurd.
I trust him, that's all.


01:28:45.500 --> 01:28:47.800
So you never wondered
how your latest fiancé


01:28:47.700 --> 01:28:51.800
ended up on the <i>Flying Dutchman</i>
in the first place?


01:29:15.100 --> 01:29:18.400
Strange to come upon
a longboat so far out in open water.


01:29:18.300 --> 01:29:21.800
Just put as many leagues behind us
as you can. As fast as you can.


01:29:21.600 --> 01:29:24.800
And what are we running from?


01:29:29.300 --> 01:29:32.100
That dress. Where did you get it?


01:29:32.100 --> 01:29:33.700
It was found aboard the ship.


01:29:33.700 --> 01:29:38.200
The crew thought it was a spirit
bringing some omen of ill-fate.


01:29:38.100 --> 01:29:41.600
- That's foolish.
- Oh, yes. Exceedingly foolish.


01:29:41.600 --> 01:29:47.600
It brought good fortune.
The spirit told us, "Pull in at Tortuga,"


01:29:47.300 --> 01:29:52.500
and we made a nice bit of profit there.
Off the books, of course.


01:29:52.400 --> 01:29:56.200
I imagine some of your crew
may have jumped ship there.


01:29:56.100 --> 01:29:59.000
- Why do you ask?
- Captain! A ship's been spotted.


01:29:59.000 --> 01:30:01.800
- Colors?
- She isn't flying any.


01:30:01.800 --> 01:30:03.800
- Pirates.
- Or worse.


01:30:14.700 --> 01:30:18.200
You will watch this.


01:30:21.200 --> 01:30:23.000
Aagh!


01:30:25.000 --> 01:30:28.000
Let no joyful voice be heard!


01:30:29.900 --> 01:30:33.100
Let no man look up at the sky with hope.


01:30:33.100 --> 01:30:34.800
Aargh!


01:30:35.200 --> 01:30:40.900
And let this day be cursed
by we who ready to wake...


01:30:42.100 --> 01:30:44.300
the kraken.


01:30:44.600 --> 01:30:46.200
No!


01:31:02.300 --> 01:31:04.900
I've doomed us all.


01:31:05.200 --> 01:31:08.100
It's the <i>Flying Dutchman!</i>


01:31:12.700 --> 01:31:17.600
Oh, Mother Carey's chickens!
What happened?
Must have hit a reef.


01:31:24.900 --> 01:31:27.900
Free the rudder!
Hard to port then hard to starboard.


01:31:27.800 --> 01:31:31.300
- Free the rudder!
- Hard to port!


01:31:38.200 --> 01:31:41.600
- Kraken!
- Kraken!


01:31:54.400 --> 01:31:57.400
Here it is! Take it!


01:31:57.400 --> 01:31:59.400
Take it!


01:32:00.300 --> 01:32:01.400
Aargh!


01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:08.000
Agh! Aaaagh!


01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:06.700
Aagh!


01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:14.000
Aaagh!


01:34:05.800 --> 01:34:10.400
The boy's not here.
He must have been claimed by the sea.


01:34:10.200 --> 01:34:12.700
I am the sea.


01:34:16.100 --> 01:34:19.300
You need time alone with your thoughts.


01:34:19.300 --> 01:34:21.900
- Brig!
- What of the survivors?


01:34:27.100 --> 01:34:30.500
There are no survivors.


01:34:49.700 --> 01:34:54.000
The chest is no longer safe.
Chart a course to Isla Cruces.


01:34:53.800 --> 01:34:58.000
Get me there first,
or there'll be the devil to pay.
"First"?


01:34:58.000 --> 01:35:01.200
Who sent that thieving charlatan
onto my ship?


01:35:01.100 --> 01:35:03.100
Who told him of the key?


01:35:06.000 --> 01:35:08.200
Jack Sparrow.


01:35:25.800 --> 01:35:32.600
My intuitive sense of the female creature
informs me that you are troubled.


01:35:32.300 --> 01:35:35.700
I just thought I'd be married by now.


01:35:35.700 --> 01:35:38.000
I'm so ready to be married.


01:35:45.100 --> 01:35:47.200
You know...


01:35:48.700 --> 01:35:54.100
Lizzie, I am captain of a ship,


01:35:54.000 --> 01:36:02.500
and being captain of a ship, I could in fact
perform a "marr-i-age" right here.


01:36:02.200 --> 01:36:04.600
Right on this deck.


01:36:04.500 --> 01:36:06.500
Right... now.


01:36:09.900 --> 01:36:12.200
No, thank you.


01:36:12.200 --> 01:36:13.800
Why not?


01:36:13.700 --> 01:36:16.800
We are very much alike, you and I.
I and you. Us.


01:36:16.700 --> 01:36:21.800
Oh. Except for a sense of honor
and decency and a moral center.


01:36:22.100 --> 01:36:24.500
And personal hygiene.


01:36:25.800 --> 01:36:28.100
Trifles.


01:36:28.100 --> 01:36:30.600
You will come over to my side. I know it.


01:36:30.600 --> 01:36:35.900
- You seem very certain.
- One word, love. Curiosity.


01:36:35.800 --> 01:36:40.500
You long for freedom.
You long to do what you want to do
because you want it.


01:36:40.500 --> 01:36:43.500
To act on selfish impulse.


01:36:43.500 --> 01:36:46.300
You want to see what it's like.


01:36:46.200 --> 01:36:50.600
One day, you won't be able to resist.


01:36:50.900 --> 01:36:53.500
Why doesn't your compass work?


01:36:55.000 --> 01:36:58.100
My compass works fine.


01:36:58.400 --> 01:37:00.100
Because you and I <i>are</i> alike,


01:37:00.100 --> 01:37:03.000
and there will come a moment
when you have a chance to show it.


01:37:03.300 --> 01:37:06.700
- To do the right thing.
- I love those moments.


01:37:07.100 --> 01:37:09.400
I like to wave at them as they pass by.


01:37:09.400 --> 01:37:15.000
You'll have the chance to do something...
something courageous.


01:37:14.700 --> 01:37:17.900
And when you do,
you'll discover something.


01:37:17.900 --> 01:37:20.500
That you're a good man.


01:37:20.500 --> 01:37:23.000
All evidence to the contrary.


01:37:22.900 --> 01:37:26.000
Oh, I have faith in you.


01:37:25.900 --> 01:37:28.800
- Want to know why?
- Do tell, dearie.


01:37:30.500 --> 01:37:33.500
Curiosity.


01:37:33.400 --> 01:37:36.300
You're going to want it.


01:37:36.300 --> 01:37:40.200
A chance to be admired
and gain the rewards that follow.


01:37:41.300 --> 01:37:43.500
You won't be able to resist.


01:37:44.700 --> 01:37:49.900
You're going to want to know
what it tastes like.


01:37:52.100 --> 01:37:55.200
I do want to know what it tastes like.


01:37:55.100 --> 01:37:56.900
But... seeing as you're a good man,


01:37:56.900 --> 01:38:01.500
I know that you'd never put me in a position
that would compromise my honor.


01:38:09.100 --> 01:38:11.300
I'm proud of you, Jack.


01:38:11.200 --> 01:38:14.100
Land, ho!


01:38:18.900 --> 01:38:20.800
I want my jar of dirt.


01:38:22.100 --> 01:38:25.100
- You're pulling too fast.
- You're pulling too slow.


01:38:24.900 --> 01:38:30.100
- We don't want the kraken to catch us.
- I'm saving me strength for when it comes.


01:38:29.900 --> 01:38:34.400
And I don't think it's "kracken" anyways.
I always heard it said "krayken."


01:38:34.300 --> 01:38:35.900
- With a long <i>a?</i>
- Uh-huh.


01:38:36.000 --> 01:38:39.900
No, no, no, no, no, no, no. "Krocken"
is how it's pronounced in Scandinavian,


01:38:39.700 --> 01:38:44.900
and "kracken" is closer to that.
We ain't Scandinavians, are we?
"Krayken."


01:38:44.900 --> 01:38:48.600
It's a mythological creature.
I can calls it what I wants.


01:38:51.200 --> 01:38:55.700
Guard the boat. Mind the tide.
Don't touch my dirt.


01:39:30.100 --> 01:39:35.300
This doesn't work. And it certainly
doesn't show you what you want most.


01:39:39.100 --> 01:39:42.500
Yes, it does.
You're sitting on it.


01:39:43.200 --> 01:39:45.000
- Beg pardon?
- Move.


01:39:52.200 --> 01:39:55.000
- "Guard the boat!" "Mind the tide!"
- I could join the circus.


01:39:54.900 --> 01:39:57.300
Mind if I shine your shoes, sir?


01:40:00.700 --> 01:40:05.400
They're here. And I cannot step foot
on land again for near of a decade.


01:40:05.300 --> 01:40:07.300
You'll trust us to act in your stead?


01:40:07.300 --> 01:40:10.800
I'll trust you to know
what awaits you should you fail!


01:40:12.100 --> 01:40:15.100
- Down, then!
- Down!


01:41:21.800 --> 01:41:23.900
It's real.


01:41:24.800 --> 01:41:26.900
You actually were telling the truth.


01:41:26.800 --> 01:41:30.400
I do that quite a lot,
yet people are always surprised.


01:41:30.300 --> 01:41:33.100
With good reason!


01:41:33.100 --> 01:41:35.600
Will!


01:41:35.600 --> 01:41:39.300
You're all right! Thank God!
I came to find you!


01:41:45.000 --> 01:41:47.200
How did you get here?


01:41:47.200 --> 01:41:51.100
Sea turtles, mate.
A pair of them, strapped to my feet.


01:41:51.000 --> 01:41:52.900
Not so easy, is it?


01:41:53.000 --> 01:41:55.600
- But I do owe you thanks, Jack.
- You do?


01:41:55.500 --> 01:41:59.600
After you tricked me onto that ship
to square your debt with Jones...


01:41:59.600 --> 01:42:01.600
- What?
- What?


01:42:02.100 --> 01:42:04.800
...I was reunited with my father.


01:42:04.800 --> 01:42:07.500
Oh, well... you're welcome, then.


01:42:07.800 --> 01:42:11.600
Everything you said to me,
every word was a lie!


01:42:11.600 --> 01:42:13.100
Pretty much.


01:42:13.100 --> 01:42:15.500
Time and tide, love.


01:42:15.400 --> 01:42:17.700
Oi! What are you doing?


01:42:18.700 --> 01:42:21.100
I'm going to kill Jones.


01:42:21.900 --> 01:42:24.200
Can't let you do that, William.


01:42:24.200 --> 01:42:30.200
'Cause if Jones is dead, who's to call
his terrible beastie off the hunt, eh?


01:42:35.800 --> 01:42:39.100
Now, if you please.


01:42:39.600 --> 01:42:42.200
The key.


01:42:44.200 --> 01:42:46.900
I keep the promises I make, Jack.


01:42:46.800 --> 01:42:50.200
I intend to free my father.
I hope you're here to see it.


01:42:50.100 --> 01:42:53.700
I can't let you do that either.
So sorry.


01:42:53.700 --> 01:42:57.300
I knew you'd warm up to me eventually.


01:42:57.900 --> 01:43:01.900
Lord Beckett desires
the content of that chest.


01:43:01.800 --> 01:43:05.000
I deliver it, I get my life back.


01:43:05.300 --> 01:43:08.000
Ah. The dark side of ambition.


01:43:08.200 --> 01:43:11.600
Oh, I prefer to see it
as the promise of redemption.


01:43:11.600 --> 01:43:12.600
Aagh!


01:43:15.600 --> 01:43:17.100
Stop it!


01:43:17.200 --> 01:43:19.100
Hyah!


01:43:23.600 --> 01:43:25.000
Will!


01:43:26.000 --> 01:43:29.100
- Guard the chest!
- No!


01:43:29.100 --> 01:43:34.000
This is barbaric!
This is no way for grown men to...


01:43:33.800 --> 01:43:39.400
Oh, fine! Let's just haul out our swords
and start banging away at each other.


01:43:39.100 --> 01:43:41.800
That will solve everything! I've had it!


01:43:41.700 --> 01:43:46.700
I've had it with wobbly-legged,
rum-soaked pirates!


01:43:49.100 --> 01:43:54.200
- How'd this go all screwy?
- Well, each wants the chest for hisself.


01:43:54.100 --> 01:43:57.300
Mr. Norrington, I think,
is trying to regain a bit of honor,


01:43:57.200 --> 01:43:59.700
old Jack's looking to trade it,
save his own skin,


01:43:59.700 --> 01:44:03.800
then Turner, there, I think he's trying
to settle some unresolved business


01:44:03.800 --> 01:44:07.400
'twixt him and his
twice-cursed pirate father.


01:44:07.300 --> 01:44:09.300
- Sad.
- This is madness!


01:44:09.300 --> 01:44:13.100
That chest must be worth
more than a shiny penny.


01:44:13.000 --> 01:44:15.000
Terrible temptation.


01:44:14.900 --> 01:44:20.900
If we was any kind of decent,
we'd remove temptation from their path.


01:44:26.100 --> 01:44:27.900
Enough!


01:44:31.200 --> 01:44:34.000
Oh. Oh!


01:44:34.000 --> 01:44:36.600
The heat!


01:44:54.700 --> 01:44:56.800
Bugger!


01:45:08.400 --> 01:45:10.900
By your leave, Mr. Turner.


01:45:55.800 --> 01:45:58.900
By <i>your</i> leave, Mr. Norrington.


01:46:48.700 --> 01:46:51.700
Do excuse me
while I kill the man who ruined my life.


01:46:51.600 --> 01:46:55.700
- Be my guest.
- Let us examine that claim for a moment.


01:46:55.900 --> 01:47:01.400
Who was it, that at the very moment you
had a notorious pirate safely behind bars,


01:47:01.200 --> 01:47:07.600
saw fit to free said pirate and
take your dearly beloved all to hisself?


01:47:07.400 --> 01:47:08.800
Eh?


01:47:08.900 --> 01:47:11.500
So whose fault is it, really,


01:47:11.500 --> 01:47:17.700
that you've ended up a rum-pot deckhand
what takes orders from pirates?
Enough!


01:47:19.800 --> 01:47:23.600
Unfortunately, Mr. Turner, he's right!


01:47:25.300 --> 01:47:27.500
Still rooting for you, mate.


01:47:33.400 --> 01:47:35.600
Oh! Oof!


01:48:02.600 --> 01:48:04.900
Aargh!


01:48:22.900 --> 01:48:25.700
We got it! We got it! We got it!


01:48:39.400 --> 01:48:42.400
Hello, poppet.


01:49:08.100 --> 01:49:10.000
Come on, run!


01:49:51.100 --> 01:49:53.100
- Sword!
- Sword! Sword!


01:49:55.800 --> 01:49:58.300
Sword!


01:50:14.300 --> 01:50:15.800
Aargh!


01:50:29.600 --> 01:50:31.200
Ugh!


01:50:37.200 --> 01:50:39.800
Follow my voice.
Follow my voice.


01:50:39.800 --> 01:50:44.100
To the left... no, turn around.
Go to the right.


01:50:44.100 --> 01:50:46.700
No, that's a tree.


01:50:46.700 --> 01:50:49.100
Oh, shut it.


01:51:35.500 --> 01:51:37.900
Jar of dirt!


01:51:45.200 --> 01:51:46.300
Argh!


01:52:35.500 --> 01:52:37.500
Right.


01:53:09.200 --> 01:53:11.500
We've got it!


01:53:20.700 --> 01:53:23.700
Come on, Turner!


01:53:39.000 --> 01:53:43.600
Leave him lie! Unless you plan
on using him to hit something with.


01:53:43.800 --> 01:53:48.500
- We're not getting out of this.
- Not with the chest.
Into the boat.


01:53:48.400 --> 01:53:51.400
- You're mad!
- Don't wait for me.


01:53:57.100 --> 01:54:00.900
- I say we respect his final wish.
- Aye!


01:54:09.800 --> 01:54:12.300
Your bravery is wasted.


01:54:13.500 --> 01:54:19.800
I shall pry the chest away
from your cold, dead hands.


01:54:21.400 --> 01:54:23.200
- Here you go!
- Oof!


01:54:34.500 --> 01:54:36.300
Pirates!


01:54:43.800 --> 01:54:45.400
Hey!


01:54:53.400 --> 01:54:56.900
- What happened to the chest?
- Norrington took it to draw them off.


01:54:57.200 --> 01:55:00.500
- You're pulling too hard!
- You ain't pulling hard enough!


01:55:00.700 --> 01:55:03.800
- Where's the commodore?
- Fell behind.


01:55:03.800 --> 01:55:05.900
My prayers be with him.


01:55:05.900 --> 01:55:07.900
Best not wallow in our grief.


01:55:07.900 --> 01:55:12.800
The bright side is you're back,
and made it off free and clear.


01:55:27.300 --> 01:55:29.600
Lord on high, deliver us.


01:55:32.700 --> 01:55:34.600
I'll handle this, mate.


01:55:36.600 --> 01:55:38.200
Oi, fishface!


01:55:39.600 --> 01:55:42.200
Lose something? Eh? <i>Scungilli!</i>


01:55:47.200 --> 01:55:49.200
Got it!


01:55:50.200 --> 01:55:54.700
Come to negotiate, eh,
have you, you slimy git?
Look what I got.


01:55:54.700 --> 01:55:57.800
# I got a jar of dirt,
I got a jar of dirt


01:55:57.700 --> 01:55:59.900
# And guess what's inside it?


01:56:00.400 --> 01:56:01.900
Enough!


01:56:09.600 --> 01:56:12.400
- Hard to starboard.
- Hard to starboard!


01:56:12.400 --> 01:56:15.500
Brace up the foreyard!


01:56:18.400 --> 01:56:20.400
Hard to starboard!


01:56:20.400 --> 01:56:25.300
Send his beloved <i>Pearl</i>
back to the depths.


01:56:36.700 --> 01:56:40.500
She's on us! She's on us!


01:56:42.000 --> 01:56:46.000
- Let them taste the triple guns.
- Aye, Captain.


01:56:57.100 --> 01:56:59.200
Aaargh!


01:57:10.700 --> 01:57:12.200
Make fast!


01:57:13.400 --> 01:57:18.600
Go on, me darling!
Show us what you've got!
Come on, girl!


01:57:24.300 --> 01:57:26.800
- She's falling behind!
- Aye. We've got her!


01:57:26.700 --> 01:57:30.400
- We're the faster?
- Against the wind, the <i>Dutchman</i> beats us.


01:57:30.300 --> 01:57:34.600
- But with the wind...
- We rob her advantage.
Aye.


01:57:36.500 --> 01:57:42.700
We're out of range!
Break off pursuit,
run her light and douse canvas!


01:57:42.600 --> 01:57:44.900
We're giving up, sir?


01:57:49.100 --> 01:57:51.000
Agh!


01:57:54.600 --> 01:57:56.900
They're giving up!


01:58:02.600 --> 01:58:04.800
My father is on that ship.


01:58:04.700 --> 01:58:07.200
We can take her.
We should turn and fight.


01:58:07.200 --> 01:58:14.200
Why fight when you can negotiate?
All one needs is the proper leverage.


01:58:14.500 --> 01:58:15.500
Oh!


01:58:23.600 --> 01:58:26.400
Where is it?
Where is the thump-thump?


01:58:27.600 --> 01:58:30.500
We must have hit a reef!


01:58:32.400 --> 01:58:36.500
No. It's not a reef!
Get away from the rail!


01:58:36.300 --> 01:58:39.400
- What is it?
- The kraken.


01:58:39.600 --> 01:58:41.900
- To arms!
- Load guns! Defend the mast!


01:58:41.900 --> 01:58:46.300
It'll attack the starboard. Run out
the cannons and hold for my signal!


01:59:18.300 --> 01:59:20.800
Easy, boys!


01:59:24.000 --> 01:59:25.000
Will?


01:59:25.400 --> 01:59:27.600
Steady! Steady.


01:59:29.500 --> 01:59:32.900
- Will!
- Hold. Hold.


01:59:32.800 --> 01:59:35.800
I think we've held fire long enough.


01:59:36.900 --> 01:59:39.300
- Will!
- Fire!


01:59:43.000 --> 01:59:44.400
Oh!


01:59:58.400 --> 02:00:02.400
- It'll be back. We have to get off the ship.
- There's no boats.


02:00:09.700 --> 02:00:14.300
Pull the grates. Get all the gunpowder
onto the net in the cargo hold!


02:00:16.400 --> 02:00:20.900
- Whatever you do, don't miss.
- As soon as you're clear.


02:00:34.300 --> 02:00:38.400
We are short-stocked on gunpowder!
Six barrels!


02:00:39.200 --> 02:00:41.300
Hurry up!


02:00:41.300 --> 02:00:44.900
- There's only six kegs of powder!
- Then load the rum.


02:00:53.200 --> 02:00:56.100
Aye, the rum too!


02:01:23.000 --> 02:01:25.000
Step to!


02:01:28.400 --> 02:01:31.000
Oh, you coward.


02:01:35.600 --> 02:01:37.600
Not good.


02:01:45.100 --> 02:01:47.900
- Haul away!
- Heave!


02:01:47.800 --> 02:01:52.200
- Heave like you're being paid for it!
- Put your backs into it!


02:02:07.700 --> 02:02:09.700
Aargh!


02:02:33.200 --> 02:02:36.500
- I got you! I got you!
- Don't let me go!


02:02:36.500 --> 02:02:39.100
- I won't let you go!
- Shoot me!


02:02:44.100 --> 02:02:47.200
- Clear?
- Higher! Higher!


02:03:03.100 --> 02:03:05.300
Here! Come on!


02:03:05.800 --> 02:03:08.500
Come and get me. I'm over here!


02:03:08.500 --> 02:03:10.500
Come on!


02:03:34.300 --> 02:03:37.600
Shoot! Elizabeth, shoot!


02:05:01.200 --> 02:05:03.200
Did we kill it?


02:05:03.100 --> 02:05:06.700
No. We just made it angry.


02:05:07.000 --> 02:05:10.000
We're not out of this yet.
Captain, orders!


02:05:11.700 --> 02:05:14.400
Abandon ship. Into the longboat.


02:05:16.500 --> 02:05:19.300
Jack. The <i>Pearl.</i>


02:05:20.400 --> 02:05:22.800
She's only a ship, mate.


02:05:22.700 --> 02:05:25.300
He's right.
We have to head for land.


02:05:25.300 --> 02:05:28.400
- It's a lot of open water.
- It's a lot of water.


02:05:28.300 --> 02:05:33.000
We have to try. We can get away
as it takes down the <i>Pearl.</i>


02:05:34.600 --> 02:05:36.400
Abandon ship.


02:05:36.400 --> 02:05:38.800
Abandon ship or abandon hope.


02:05:54.800 --> 02:05:56.900
Thank you, Jack.


02:05:58.800 --> 02:06:01.600
We're not free yet, love.


02:06:01.500 --> 02:06:03.700
You came back.


02:06:06.100 --> 02:06:08.800
I always knew you were a good man.


02:06:21.200 --> 02:06:26.400
Prepare to cast off.
There's no time to lose.
Come on, Will. Step to!


02:06:44.600 --> 02:06:47.000
It's after you, not the ship.


02:06:47.000 --> 02:06:49.900
It's not us.


02:06:49.900 --> 02:06:52.700
This is the only way, don't you see?


02:06:55.700 --> 02:06:57.900
I'm not sorry.


02:07:03.200 --> 02:07:05.200
Pirate.


02:07:23.600 --> 02:07:25.800
Where's Jack?


02:07:29.200 --> 02:07:33.200
He elected to stay behind
to give us a chance.


02:07:41.400 --> 02:07:43.200
Go!


02:07:55.400 --> 02:07:58.800
Bugger. Bugger, bugger,
bugger, bugger, bugger!


02:08:25.800 --> 02:08:27.900
Come on!


02:08:31.900 --> 02:08:34.600
Just got it. Come on.


02:09:02.400 --> 02:09:04.900
Not so bad.


02:09:06.100 --> 02:09:07.200
Oh.


02:09:15.600 --> 02:09:17.500
Hello, beastie.


02:10:04.200 --> 02:10:08.400
Jack Sparrow, our debt is settled.


02:10:09.500 --> 02:10:15.800
The captain goes down with his ship.
Turns out not even Jack Sparrow
can best the devil.


02:10:20.700 --> 02:10:23.300
Open the chest.


02:10:24.200 --> 02:10:26.800
Open the chest. I need to see it!


02:10:41.600 --> 02:10:47.600
Damn you, Jack Sparrow!


02:10:52.900 --> 02:10:55.700
The last of our ships is returned.


02:10:55.700 --> 02:10:58.100
Is there any news on the chest?


02:10:58.100 --> 02:11:03.700
None. But one of the ships
did pick up a man adrift at sea.


02:11:03.500 --> 02:11:05.700
He had these.


02:11:12.400 --> 02:11:15.500
I took the liberty of filling in my name.


02:11:22.500 --> 02:11:26.600
If you intend to claim these,
then you must have something to trade.


02:11:26.600 --> 02:11:29.200
Do you have the compass?


02:11:29.200 --> 02:11:31.000
Better.


02:11:37.800 --> 02:11:40.400
The heart of Davy Jones.


02:12:33.400 --> 02:12:35.900
Against the cold...


02:12:35.900 --> 02:12:38.200
and the sorrow.


02:12:48.000 --> 02:12:50.600
It's a shame.


02:12:50.500 --> 02:12:53.000
I know you're thinking that with the <i>Pearl</i>


02:12:53.000 --> 02:12:58.400
you could have captured the devil
and set free your father's soul.


02:12:58.200 --> 02:13:01.000
Doesn't matter now.


02:13:00.800 --> 02:13:03.500
The <i>Pearl</i> is gone,


02:13:03.500 --> 02:13:05.400
along with its captain.


02:13:05.400 --> 02:13:07.600
Aye.


02:13:07.700 --> 02:13:11.600
And already the world
seems a bit less bright.


02:13:11.500 --> 02:13:14.600
He fooled us all right till the end,


02:13:14.600 --> 02:13:18.400
but I guess that honest streak
finally won out.


02:13:20.900 --> 02:13:22.700
To Jack Sparrow.


02:13:22.700 --> 02:13:27.700
- Never another like Captain Jack.
- He was a gentleman of fortune, he was.


02:13:28.100 --> 02:13:30.100
He was a good man.


02:13:49.900 --> 02:13:53.900
If there was anything could be done
to bring him back...


02:13:55.100 --> 02:13:57.500
- Elizabeth.
- Would you do it?


02:13:57.400 --> 02:13:58.700
Hm?


02:13:59.800 --> 02:14:02.200
What would you...


02:14:02.100 --> 02:14:03.800
Hm?


02:14:05.400 --> 02:14:09.400
What would any of you
be willing to do, hm?


02:14:10.300 --> 02:14:14.300
Would you sail to the ends of the Earth
and beyond


02:14:14.100 --> 02:14:19.200
to fetch back witty Jack
and him precious <i>Pearl?</i>


02:14:25.000 --> 02:14:26.200
Aye.


02:14:27.200 --> 02:14:28.500
Aye.


02:14:29.100 --> 02:14:30.300
Aye.


02:14:30.800 --> 02:14:32.500
Aye.


02:14:35.300 --> 02:14:37.400
Yes.


02:14:39.500 --> 02:14:40.700
Aye.


02:14:40.800 --> 02:14:42.800
All right.


02:14:43.700 --> 02:14:50.700
But if you go and brave the weird
and haunted shores at world's end...


02:14:51.500 --> 02:14:57.800
then you will need a captain
who knows those waters.


02:15:11.900 --> 02:15:16.000
So tell me,
what's become of my ship?


02:15:17.000 --> 00:00:00.000
